+## 17. X2 T1: exec-equivalent job control for `run` (Unix)
+
+Task: make `heimdall-proto run` indistinguishable from plain exec in five job-control situations (S1–S5 of the X2
+task), on darwin-arm64 and linux-arm64, under bash and zsh. Code: `src/runner.rs` only (Unix path; the Windows
+path is unchanged and still compiles).
+
+### Before → after
+
+| Scenario | Before (starting commit) | Cause | Change |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| S1 non-interactive parent, no tty on stdio; a third party SIGSTOPs/SIGTSTPs the child, then SIGCONTs the child only | launcher mirrored the stop onto itself and was never continued: it never reaped the child or exited (the §9.5 finding) | the stop mirror ran in every setting | the stop is mirrored only in a job-control setting: the controlling terminal is on stdio, or its foreground group is the launcher's or the child's (found through `/dev/tty` when stdio is redirected). Otherwise the launcher just keeps waiting, as plain exec's caller would |
+| S2 interactive shell; third party stops the foreground child, then continues the child only | already passed via the permitted deviation (b): notice and `$?` as plain exec, job listed stopped until `fg`, `fg` → child's status | a stopped launcher cannot observe the child being continued | unchanged (deviation b, see below) |
+| S3 child self-SIGTSTP, then a third party SIGCONTs the **launcher** only | launcher ran again but the child stayed stopped; the shell listed the job running while the child was stopped | SIGCONT was not observed | SIGCONT is now caught (signal-hook; its continue action is unaffected) and passed on to the child while the child is known stopped. The child then reads the terminal in the background and SIGTTIN stops the whole group, as under plain exec |
+| S4 nested `bash -i` under `run` runs `suspend`; outer `fg` | after `fg` the nested shell (own process group, still stopped, terminal given to the launcher's group) never answered | the outer `fg` continues and gives the terminal to the launcher's group only | at the stop, the launcher records whether the child's own group held the terminal. On SIGCONT, if the launcher's group now holds it, it hands the terminal back to that group (`tcsetpgrp`, only while the launcher is in the foreground) and continues that group, then the child |
+| S5 SIGSTOP (self or third party) with `--meta-file` | already passed | — | unchanged |
+
+Also changed: the 200 ms stale-stop grace (§15, Q18) now restarts whenever the launcher itself receives SIGCONT
+during it, so time spent stopped (Ctrl-Z of the group, SIGTTIN of the group) never counts towards the grace, and
+the stop is re-checked right before the launcher stops itself.
+
+### Mechanism now used (`run`, Unix)
+§15's order, plus: SIGCONT joins the caught set (unless inherited ignored, K6a); a mutex-protected `JobState`
+(`exited`, `child_stopped`, `restore_fg`, `conts`) is shared by the wait loop and the forwarder. The forwarder
+signals the child only while holding the lock with `exited == false` (no recycled-PID signal). The launcher still
+never calls `setpgid`, never stops anything but its own PID, and calls `tcsetpgrp` only to give the terminal back to
+a child group that had taken it itself, and only while its own group is the foreground group (K6a).
+
